ment or cover a distance in the shortest possible time • **Purpose** To assess rapid movement and sprinting ability • **Assessment tests** • 30 metre sprint test • 40 metre sprint test • 10 metre sprint test • **Parameters** Time taken to complete the distance • **Example** 30 metre sprint test for sprinters and football players --- **5 Agility** • **Definition** Ability to change body direction rapidly and efficiently while maintaining balance and control • **Purpose** To assess quick directional changes during sports activities • **Assessment tests** • T test • Illinois agility test • 505 agility test • **Parameters** Time taken movement control and directional changes • **Example** T test for basketball players --- **6 Power** • **Definition** Ability to produce maximum force in minimum time Power is the product of force and velocity • **Purpose** To assess explosive muscular performance • **Assessment tests** • Vertical jump test • Standing broad jump test • Medicine ball throw test • **Parameters** Jump height distance or throwing distance • **Example** Vertical jump test for volleyball players --- **7 Balance** • **Definition** Ability to maintain the bodys centre of mass over its base of support • **Types** Static balance and dynamic balance • **Purpose** To assess postural control and stability during sports activities • **Assessment tests** • Single leg stance test • Y Balance test • Star Excursion Balance Test SEBT • **Parameters** Time maintained reach distance and postural stability • **Example** Y Balance test for football players --- **8 Coordination** • **Definition** Ability to perform smooth accurate and controlled movements using different body parts • **Purpose** To assess neuromuscular control and the integration of movements • **Assessment tests** • Alternate hand wall toss test • Finger to nose test • Alternate hand foot tapping test • **Parameters** Accuracy timing rhythm and movement smoothness • **Example** Wall toss test for tennis players 